# services/box/ops/backup.env.example
# Deployed to the box as /etc/agent-box/backup.env (root-only, 0400) and sourced by
# backup.sh. Copy this, fill the real values at deploy time. NO secrets live in here —
# the restic password is a separate root-only file (RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE).

# --- Hetzner Storage Box (different region than the box: Falkenstein/Nuremberg) ---
STORAGEBOX_HOST=uNNNNNN.your-storagebox.de
STORAGEBOX_USER=uNNNNNN
STORAGEBOX_PORT=23                          # Hetzner Storage Box SSH/SFTP port is 23
STORAGEBOX_SSH_KEY=/root/.ssh/storagebox_append   # the APPEND-ONLY key (cannot delete)

# The repo path is the one pinned by the forced `rclone serve restic` command in the
# Storage Box authorized_keys (see Stage 1.5 plan Task 2). restic talks to it over the
# rclone backend, so the repository string is "rclone:<that path>".
RESTIC_REPO_PATH=box-backup
# Persistent, root-only file on the LUKS-encrypted disk (survives the per-boot unlock).
# Encrypted at rest by LUKS. Migrate to systemd-creds LoadCredentialEncrypted later.
RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE=/etc/agent-box/restic-password

# Existing Eve file/blob volumes retained during keeper adoption. Exact names only,
# whitespace-separated; do not include the Postgres data volume. Required for a
# complete restore when lifecycle.bindings.*.workflowVolume is configured.
# LARES_WORKFLOW_VOLUMES="installation_example_workflow_files"
# /etc/lares is included in the encrypted secrets bundle, /srv/lares in restic data.
# AGE_SECRETS_RECIPIENT=age1...   # required once /etc/lares exists; private key off-box

# --- LAR-54-s6: an alternative target — any restic repository, not only a Storage Box ---
# The design decision (docs/specs/2026-09-03-backup-and-restore-per-installation-design.md,
# 2026-09-04): the target is a REQUIRED install-time choice with three supported
# shapes, all restic — an SFTP host the owner controls, an S3-compatible EU bucket, or
# a local path on a second disk. Setting RESTIC_REPOSITORY below switches backup.sh,
# backup-verify.sh and restore-drill.sh to `restic -r "$RESTIC_REPOSITORY"` and drops
# the STORAGEBOX_*/rclone form above entirely (leave RESTIC_REPOSITORY unset to keep
# that form). Only ONE of the examples below applies at a time. The matching
# credentials are restic's own standard environment variables — never put a
# credential inside RESTIC_REPOSITORY itself beyond what restic's own repository
# syntax requires, and never in RESTIC_REPO_PATH above.
#
# 1) SFTP — any host the owner controls (a second Hetzner Storage Box, a NAS, a
#    friend's server). Auth is the SSH agent/keys already on the box; no password
#    belongs in the URL.
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=sftp:backup-user@sftp.example.org:/restic-repo
#
# 2) S3-compatible, EU-region only (Scaleway, Hetzner Object Storage, a self-hosted
#    MinIO) — never a US bucket; the vendor policy is European-only infrastructure.
#    Credentials go in restic's own AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID/AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, not in
#    the repository string.
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=s3:https://s3.eu-example.your-provider.example/restic-repo
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=REPLACE-WITH-KEY-ID
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=REPLACE-WITH-SECRET-KEY
#
# 3) Local path, on a SECOND disk — never the box's own boot/data disk.
#    WARNING (the 2026-09-04 decision, written here on purpose): one machine is not a
#    backup. A local-path target protects against a failed drive or an accidental
#    `rm -rf`, but not against the box itself being lost, stolen, seized or destroyed
#    (fire, flood, theft) — the ENTIRE POINT of a backup living somewhere else. Accept
#    this only as a stopgap for a hobbyist install with no second machine yet, and
#    plan to move to (1) or (2) — the installer reminds the owner of this monthly.
# RESTIC_REPOSITORY=/mnt/second-disk/restic-repo

# --- healthchecks.io ---
# Pinged by backup-verify.sh (NOT by backup.sh), and only after it has read a fresh
# nightly snapshot back OUT of the restic repository. A missed ping alerts.
#
# REQUIRED. backup-verify.sh refuses to run with this empty, on purpose: on the
# deployed box this line was blank and backup.sh's `if [ -n "$HC_URL" ]` skipped the
# ping in silence, which is why three failed nights in August 2026 went unnoticed
# (ORB-150). An unconfigured heartbeat is the same silence as no heartbeat.
#
# The check to create in healthchecks.io: period 1 day, grace 2h, notifying
# #lares-operations (a failed backup is degradation needing action, not an
# interrupt — ADR-0012 rule 1).
HC_URL=https://hc-ping.com/REPLACE-WITH-UUID

# --- backup-verify.sh tuning (optional; defaults shown) ---
# How old the newest nightly snapshot may be before it counts as stale. The backup
# runs 03:00 UTC and the verifier 05:00 UTC, so a backup that failed last night
# leaves a ~26h-old snapshot and is caught the same morning.
# BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS=24
# The path whose presence proves the snapshot carries the databases. 31 consecutive
# snapshots in July 2026 were green and contained no dump at all.
# BACKUP_REQUIRED_PATH=/var/backups/pg

# --- restore-drill.sh tuning (optional; defaults shown) ---
# Where the monthly drill restores a snapshot before loading its dumps. NEVER /tmp:
# on the box /tmp can be a small tmpfs (RAM), and a full restore (the vault,
# /opt/agent-box and every database dump) needs real disk. /var/tmp is disk-backed
# on Linux by convention.
# DRILL_SCRATCH_ROOT=/var/tmp
# The drill refuses to run if DRILL_SCRATCH_ROOT has less free space than this,
# rather than guessing how big the restore will be.
# DRILL_MIN_FREE_MB=5120
# The non-database directories the drill also proves come back readable, restored
# alongside the Postgres dumps. Whitespace-separated.
# DRILL_RESTORE_PATHS="/srv/lares /opt/agent-box"
# How much smaller than live a restored database's table count may be and still
# count as a pass — a table created seconds after last night's dump is not a
# broken restore, but a restore missing most of its tables is.
# DRILL_TABLE_TOLERANCE_PCT=90
# Read by backup-verify.sh (LAR-54-s4), not restore-drill.sh itself: how many days a
# drill may go without a pass before the nightly alarm treats the backup as unproven.
# The drill runs monthly, so 45 gives it a full cycle plus real slack.
# DRILL_MAX_AGE_DAYS=45
